{"id":"neet-2026-q041","pn":41,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Conductors and electrostatic shielding","q":"Which of the following statements are correct?\nA. Inside a conductor, the electrostatic field is zero.\nB. Electric field at the surface of a charged conductor does not depend on its surface charge density.\nC. The interior of a charged conductor can have no excess charge in the static situation.\nD. At the surface of a charged conductor, the electrostatic field must be normal to the surface at every point.\nE. The electrostatic potential is zero everywhere inside a charged conductor.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["C, D and E only","A, B and D only","A, C and D only","A, C and E only"],"c":2,"s":"A is correct: the electrostatic field is zero inside a conductor.\nB is wrong: the field at the surface is (σ/ε₀)n, so it depends on the surface charge density σ.\nC is correct: the interior of a charged conductor cannot have excess charge in the static situation.\nD is correct: at the surface the field is perpendicular to the surface.\nE is wrong: the potential is constant inside, and it can be non-zero."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q028","pn":28,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Coulomb's law","q":"Two identical charged conducting spheres A and B have their centres separated by a certain distance. Charge on each sphere is q and the force of repulsion between them is F. A third identical uncharged conducting sphere is brought in contact with sphere A first and then with B and finally removed from both. New force of repulsion between spheres A and B (Radii of A and B are negligible compared to the distance of separation so that for calculating force between them they can be considered as point charges) is best given as:","o":["3F/5","2F/3","F/2","3F/8"],"c":3,"s":"Initially each sphere has charge q at separation r, so F = Kqq/r².\nThe third sphere touches A: each gets q/2, so A has q/2.\nThen it (with q/2) touches B (with q): total 3q/2 shared equally, so each gets 3q/4.\nNow A has q/2 and B has 3q/4.\nF′ = K(q/2)(3q/4)/r².\nF′ = 3F/8.","sfig":{"w":210,"h":190,"alt":"Two charged spheres A and B a distance r apart: first both q, then A has q/2 and B has 3q/4","d":[["t",34,16,"A","m"],["t",172,16,"B","m"],["c",34,43,18],["c",172,43,18],["l",52,43,154,43],["t",100,57,"r","m"],["t",34,47,"q","ms"],["t",100,57,"r","m"],["t",172,47,"q","ms"],["t",34,136,"A","m"],["t",172,136,"B","m"],["c",34,163,18],["c",172,163,18],["l",52,163,154,163],["t",100,177,"r","m"],["t",34,167,"q/2","ms"],["t",172,167,"3q/4","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q035","pn":35,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Electric dipole in a uniform field","q":"An electric dipole with dipole moment 5 × 10⁻⁶ Cm is aligned with the direction of a uniform electric field of magnitude 4 × 10⁵ N/C. The dipole is then rotated through an angle of 60° with respect to the electric field. The change in the potential energy of the dipole is:","o":["0.8 J","1.0 J","1.2 J","1.5 J"],"c":1,"s":"Given |p| = 5 × 10⁻⁶ C m and |E| = 4 × 10⁵ N/C.\nθᵢ = 0° and θ_f = 60°.\nΔU = U_f − Uᵢ.\n= −PE cosθ_f + PE cosθᵢ.\n= PE[cosθᵢ − cosθ_f].\n= 5 × 10⁻⁶ × 4 × 10⁵ [1 − 1/2].\n= 10 × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁵ = 1 J."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q005","pn":5,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Torque on dipole","q":"An electric dipole is placed at an angle of 30° with an electric field of intensity 2 × 10⁵ NC⁻¹. It experiences a torque equal to 4 Nm. Calculate the magnitude of charge on the dipole, if the dipole length is 2 cm.","o":["2 mC","8 mC","6 mC","4 mC"],"c":0,"s":"Given E = 2 × 10⁵ N/C, length = 2 cm = 0.02 m, torque = 4 Nm.\nTorque = pE sinθ.\n4 = p × 2 × 10⁵ × sin30°, so p = 4 × 10⁻⁵ C m.\nCharge q = p/length = (4 × 10⁻⁵)/0.02 = 2 × 10⁻³ C = 2 mC."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q024","pn":24,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Gauss's law","q":"If ∮E·dS = 0 over a surface, then :","o":["the electric field inside the surface is necessarily uniform.","the number of flux lines entering the surface must be equal to the number of flux lines leaving it.","the magnitude of electric field on the surface is constant.","all the charges must necessarily be inside the surface."],"c":1,"s":"∮E·dS = 0 means the net flux through the surface is zero.\nSo the number of flux lines entering the surface equals the number leaving it."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q042","pn":42,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Coulomb force vs gravitation","q":"The ratio of Coulomb's electrostatic force to the gravitational force between an electron and a proton separated by some distance is 2.4 × 10³⁹. The ratio of the proportionality constant, K = 1/(4πε₀) to the Gravitational constant G is nearly (Given that the charge of the proton and electron each = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, the mass of the electron = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg, the mass of the proton = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg):","o":["10","10²⁰","10³⁰","10⁴⁰"],"c":1,"s":"F_E/F_G = Kq²/(G m₁m₂); the distance cancels.\nSo K/G = (F_E/F_G) × m₁m₂/q².\nm₁m₂ = 1.67×10⁻²⁷ × 9.11×10⁻³¹ ≈ 15.2×10⁻⁵⁸ and q² = 2.56×10⁻³⁸.\nK/G = 2.4×10³⁹ × 15.2×10⁻⁵⁸ / 2.56×10⁻³⁸ ≈ 1.4×10²⁰ ≈ 10²⁰."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q047","pn":47,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Field of an electric dipole at large distance","q":"Two point charges −q and +q are placed at a distance of L, as shown in the figure. The magnitude of electric field intensity at a distance R (R >> L) varies as:","o":["1/R³","1/R⁴","1/R⁶","1/R²"],"c":0,"s":"For R >> L the pair acts as an electric dipole.\nThe field is E = 2p/(4πε₀R³) (on the axis), with p = qL.\nSo E ∝ 1/R³.","fig":{"w":280,"h":88,"alt":"A line joining a charge -q at the left end and +q at the right end, with a double-headed arrow below marking the separation L.","d":[["c",30,20,4,"F"],["c",250,20,4,"F"],["l",30,20,250,20],["t",30,44,"−q","m"],["t",250,44,"+q","m"],["l",140,62,32,62,"a"],["l",140,62,248,62,"a"],["t",140,80,"L","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2021-q002","pn":2,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Polar molecules","q":"Polar molecules are the molecules","o":["Having a permanent electric dipole moment","Having zero dipole moment","Acquire a dipole moment only in the presence of electric field due to displacement of charges","Acquire a dipole moment only when magnetic field is absent"],"c":0,"s":"In a polar molecule the centre of positive charge and the centre of negative charge do not coincide.\nSo it has a dipole moment even with no field."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q011","pn":11,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Dipole in non-uniform field","q":"A dipole is placed in an electric field as shown. In which direction will it move?","o":["Towards the right as its potential energy will increase.","Towards the left as its potential energy will increase.","Towards the right as its potential energy will decrease.","Towards the left as its potential energy will decrease."],"c":2,"s":"The dipole moment points from –q to +q, so p points left while E points right. The angle is 180°.\nU = –pE cos 180° = +pE, the highest value.\nThe field lines spread out to the right, so E gets weaker there.\nMoving right lowers E, and so lowers U; a system moves to lower energy.\nTorque is zero at 180°, but there is a net force to the right.\nSo it moves right and U decreases.","fig":{"w":240,"h":190,"alt":"Dipole with +q left and -q right on the horizontal field line E, where the field lines spread out to the right","d":[["l",20,95,164,95,"da"],["l",20,95,136,11,"da"],["l",20,95,136,179,"da"],["c",66,86,1.6,"F"],["c",110,86,1.6,"F"],["l",66,86,110,86],["t",66,108,"+q","ms"],["t",110,108,"–q","ms"],["t",170,99,"E"],["p","M169 87 H180 M177 85 L180 87 L177 89"]]},"sfig":{"w":240,"h":190,"alt":"Same field lines with the dipole moment p pointing left, opposite to E","d":[["l",20,95,164,95,"a"],["l",20,95,136,17,"a"],["l",20,95,136,173,"a"],["c",64,95,1.6,"F"],["c",104,95,1.6,"F"],["t",64,110,"+q","ms"],["t",104,110,"–q","ms"],["l",100,84,70,84,"a"],["t",85,76,"P","m"],["t",170,99,"E"],["p","M79 63 H90 M87 61 L90 63 L87 65"],["p","M169 87 H180 M177 85 L180 87 L177 89"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2020-q107","pn":107,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Field of a charged sphere","q":"A spherical conductor of radius 10 cm has a charge of 3.2 × 10⁻⁷ C distributed uniformly. What is the magnitude of electric field at a point 15 cm from the centre of the sphere ? (1/(4πε₀) = 9 × 10⁹ N m²/C²)","o":["1.28 × 10⁵ N/C","1.28 × 10⁶ N/C","1.28 × 10⁷ N/C","1.28 × 10⁴ N/C"],"c":0,"s":"The point is outside the sphere, so it acts like a point charge at the centre.\nE = kQ/r² = (9 × 10⁹ × 3.2 × 10⁻⁷)/(0.15)².\n= 2880/0.0225 = 1.28 × 10⁵ N/C."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q103","pn":103,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Field of a charged shell","q":"A hollow metal sphere of radius R is uniformly charged. The electric field due to the sphere at a distance r from the centre :","o":["zero as r increases for r < R, increases as r increases for r > R","decreases as r increases for r < R and for r > R","increases as r increases for r < R and for r > R","zero as r increases for r < R, decreases as r increases for r > R"],"c":3,"s":"Inside a hollow charged shell (r < R) the field is zero.\nAt the surface E = kQ/R², its maximum value.\nOutside (r > R) E = kQ/r², so E falls as r increases.\nSo the field is zero inside and decreases with r outside.","sfig":{"w":170,"h":170,"alt":"A circle representing the hollow metal sphere with plus signs spread evenly round its outside.","d":[["c",85,85,42],["t",85,31,"+","ms"],["t",104.8,34.5,"+","ms"],["t",122.3,44.6,"+","ms"],["t",135.2,60,"+","ms"],["t",142.1,78.9,"+","ms"],["t",142.1,99.1,"+","ms"],["t",135.2,118,"+","ms"],["t",122.3,133.4,"+","ms"],["t",104.8,143.5,"+","ms"],["t",85,147,"+","ms"],["t",65.2,143.5,"+","ms"],["t",47.7,133.4,"+","ms"],["t",34.8,118,"+","ms"],["t",27.9,99.1,"+","ms"],["t",27.9,78.9,"+","ms"],["t",34.8,60,"+","ms"],["t",47.7,44.6,"+","ms"],["t",65.2,34.5,"+","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2019-q110","pn":110,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Field of line charges","q":"Two parallel infinite line charges with linear charge densities +λ C/m and –λ C/m are placed at a distance of 2R in free space. What is the electric field mid-way between the two line charges?","o":["λ/(π ε₀ R) N/C","λ/(2π ε₀ R) N/C","zero","2λ/(π ε₀ R) N/C"],"c":0,"s":"Field of a line charge at distance R is 2kλ/R = λ/(2π ε₀ R).\nMid-way, the +λ line pushes away and the −λ line pulls towards itself, so both fields point the same way.\nTotal E = 2 × λ/(2π ε₀ R) = λ/(π ε₀ R).","sfig":{"w":240,"h":130,"alt":"Two parallel vertical line charges, λ on the left and −λ on the right; point P midway on a horizontal dashed line, distance R from each, with E₁ and E₂ both pointing right.","d":[["l",30,30,30,115],["l",210,30,210,115],["t",30,22,"λ","m"],["t",210,22,"−λ","m"],["l",30,75,210,75,"d"],["l",120,75,150,75,"a"],["l",150,75,180,75,"a"],["c",120,75,2.5,"F"],["t",112,64,"P","e"],["t",135,64,"E₁","m"],["t",165,64,"E₂","m"],["t",75,94,"R","m"],["t",165,94,"R","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2019-q133","pn":133,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Coulomb's law","q":"Two point charges A and B, having charges +Q and –Q respectively, are placed at certain distance apart and force acting between them is F. If 25 % charge of A is transferred to B, then force between the charges becomes-","o":["16F/9","4F/3","F","9F/16"],"c":3,"s":"Originally F = kQ²/r².\n25% of Q = Q/4 moves from A to B.\nA now has 3Q/4. B had −Q and now has −Q + Q/4 = −3Q/4.\nF′ = k(3Q/4)(3Q/4)/r² = (9/16) kQ²/r² = 9F/16."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q020","pn":20,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Electric Charges and Fields","st":"Motion of charges in electric field","q":"A electron falls from rest through a vertical distance h in a uniform and vertically upward directed electric field E. The direction of electric field in now reversed, keeping its magnitude the same. A proton is allowed to fall from rest in it through the same vertical distance h. The time of fall of the electron, in comparison to the time of fall of the proton is","o":["smaller","5 times greater","10 times greater","equal"],"c":0,"s":"Force magnitude on each is eE, the same.\nAcceleration a = (eE)/(m), so a ∝ (1)/(m).\nElectron is much lighter, so its acceleration is larger.\nFrom h = (1)/(2)at², t = √((2h)/(a)), larger a means smaller t.\nSo the electron takes less time."}
